msgctxt "doeAnalysis|"
msgid ""
"Finds the factor settings that maximize the composite desirability of the "
"included responses, based on the fitted model of each response. Continuous "
"factors are searched within their observed range; all combinations of "
"discrete factor levels are tried."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeAnalysis|"
msgid ""
"The responses to optimize. For each response, the individual desirability "
"(between 0 and 1) is set by the goal, bounds, target and weight. The "
"composite desirability is the weighted geometric mean of the individual "
"desirabilities, with the importances (normalized to sum to 1) as weights."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeAnalysis|"
msgid "Whether to maximize or minimize the response, or to hit a target value."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeAnalysis|"
msgid ""
"Response value below which the individual desirability is 0. Used for the "
"goals maximize and target. Only editable with 'Set all bounds manually'; "
"otherwise the minimum of the observed response is used."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeAnalysis|"
msgid ""
"Response value at which the individual desirability is 1. Always editable "
"for the goal target. For maximize and minimize only editable with 'Set all "
"targets manually'; otherwise the maximum or minimum of the observed response "
"is used."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeAnalysis|"
msgid ""
"Response value above which the individual desirability is 0. Used for the "
"goals minimize and target. Only editable with 'Set all bounds manually'; "
"otherwise the maximum of the observed response is used."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeAnalysis|"
msgid ""
"Shape of the individual desirability between bound and target (0.1 to 10). "
"With 1 it increases linearly; larger values require the response to be "
"closer to the target for a high desirability, smaller values are more "
"lenient."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeAnalysis|"
msgid ""
"Relative importance of the response in the composite desirability (0.1 to "
"10). Only enabled with more than one response."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeAnalysis|"
msgid ""
"Show the optimizer settings per response and the optimal solution: the "
"factor levels with the highest composite desirability, that desirability, "
"and the predicted (fitted) responses at these levels."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeAnalysis|"
msgid "Confidence and prediction intervals"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeAnalysis|"
msgid ""
"Predicted response at the optimal (or manually set) factor levels, with the "
"confidence interval for the mean response and the prediction interval for a "
"single new run. Blocks are averaged out; covariates are held at their mean."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeAnalysis|"
msgid ""
"Show a matrix of plots with one column per factor. The top row shows the "
"composite desirability, the other rows one per response. Each plot varies "
"one factor while the other factors are held at the optimal (or manually set) "
"levels, which are marked in red."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeAnalysis|"
msgid ""
"What the response rows of the optimization plot show: the predicted response "
"or its individual desirability."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeAnalysis|"
msgid ""
"Draw the optimization plot at manually entered factor levels instead of the "
"optimal solution. Enter a value for every factor; discrete factors need one "
"of their observed levels."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeAnalysis|"
msgid ""
"Use the entered lower and upper bounds instead of the minimum and maximum of "
"the observed responses."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeAnalysis|"
msgid ""
"Use the entered targets for the goals maximize and minimize instead of the "
"maximum or minimum of the observed responses."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeAnalysis|"
msgid ""
"Select the method used to calculate the bin widths of the residual "
"histogram, or specify a manual number of bins."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeAnalysis|"
msgid "Number of bins when the bin width type is set to manual."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeAnalysis|"
msgid ""
"How the sum of squares of each term in the ANOVA table is calculated. The "
"types differ in which other terms a term is adjusted for. In balanced "
"designs with orthogonal (coded) terms, such as complete two-level factorial "
"designs, all types give the same result. In unbalanced designs, e.g. with "
"missing runs, covariates or squared terms, they can differ. The Model, Error "
"and Total rows are the same for all types. See the description of the ANOVA "
"table below."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeAnalysis|"
msgid ""
"Sequential sums of squares: each term is adjusted only for the terms entered "
"before it, so the results depend on the order of the terms. The term rows "
"add up to the Model row."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeAnalysis|"
msgid ""
"Each term is adjusted for all other terms, except for higher-order terms "
"that contain it. For example, A is adjusted for B, but not for A✻B."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeAnalysis|"
msgid ""
"Each term is adjusted for all other terms in the model, i.e. it measures "
"what the term adds when it is entered last. Results for lower-order terms "
"depend on how the factors are coded. Not available when some coefficients "
"are aliased."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeAnalysis|"
msgid ""
"Calculate the ANOVA table from the factors in coded units (-1 to 1), so that "
"squared terms are calculated from coded values. Only has an effect when the "
"results are not displayed in coded units. It can change the type II and III "
"sums of squares of the linear terms."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeFactorial|"
msgid ""
"A factorial design is an experimental setup used to study the effects of "
"multiple factors by varying them simultaneously. Each factor is tested at "
"different levels and in different combinations with other factors. This "
"design allows for the examination of both the main effects of each factor "
"and the interactions between factors. It provides a systematic approach to "
"understanding how different variables influence the outcome."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeFactorial|"
msgid "Set the total number of factors in the design."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "doeFactorial|"
msgid ""
"Set the maximum number of levels for the factors in the design. Only "
"applicable for general full factorial designs, else factors always have two "
"levels. If a factor has fewer levels than the maximum number of levels in "
"the design, leave the remaining level cells empty or add a space."
msgstr ""

